# Wawona Swift / SwiftUI backports

Apply this rule to every Wawona organization repository that ships Swift or
SwiftUI. Follow Dave DeLong's Backport technique:
https://davedelong.com/blog/2021/10/09/simplifying-backwards-compatibility-in-swift/

## Non-negotiable rules

1. Do not scatter `if #available` through SwiftUI feature views. Put
   availability checks in the shared `Backport<Content>` namespace and use
   `.backport` or `Backport<Any>` at call sites.
2. Every shim must call the native API on newer OSes and provide an honest
   Wawona-owned fallback on every older supported OS. No-op is allowed only for
   optional decoration and must be documented.
3. Match system API names and call shapes where practical so usages stay
   searchable and deleting `.backport` is mechanical when support advances.
4. Extend the shared compatibility source
   (`Sources/WawonaUI/Backport.swift` in Wawona); do not create feature-local
   wrappers.
5. Backport new types with a nested type or static `@ViewBuilder` factory.
   When environment/property-wrapper types differ, define one stable
   Wawona-owned abstraction; never force-cast to fake compatibility.
6. Lifecycle, UIKit/AppKit, Metal, scene, and delegate availability belongs in
   centralized platform adapters when it is not a SwiftUI concern.
7. Backport does not lower a binary's OS requirement. Keep XcodeGen, SwiftPM,
   Rust target triples, Nix recipes, embedded frameworks, and transitive native
   dependencies aligned with the claimed deployment floor.
8. Prove both paths: compile at the oldest supported target, run its fallback,
   and run the native path on the newest OS. Without oldest-runtime evidence,
   the path is wired, not supported.

Required base:

```swift
public struct Backport<Content> {
    public let content: Content
    public init(_ content: Content) { self.content = content }
}

public extension View {
    var backport: Backport<Self> { Backport(self) }
}
```

Review all changed feature views for `#available` and audit unguarded newer
types (`App`, `StateObject`, `NavigationStack`, environment values, scene APIs).
Do not change Wawona's production `WWNCore*` C polling FFI for UI backports.

